<section id="concepts">
<h2>Core concepts</h2>
<p class="lead">The ideas that make this architecture secure, in the order a request meets them. Route 53 first resolves the name to the load balancer’s address; the request itself then enters through the Internet Gateway.</p>
<div class="flow"><span>Internet</span><b>→</b><span>Internet Gateway</span><b>→</b><span class="o">Load Balancer + WAF</span><b>→</b><span>Private compute</span><b>→</b><span class="o">Private data</span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><div class="ico">🛡️</div><h3>Defense in depth</h3><ul><li>Edge: Route 53, WAF, CloudFront</li><li>Network: VPC, subnets, routing</li><li>Traffic: security groups, NACLs</li><li>Identity: IAM roles and policies</li><li>Data: encryption, backups, Multi-AZ</li></ul></div>
<div class="card"><div class="ico">🌐</div><h3>Public vs private</h3><p>Only load balancers and NAT gateways sit in public subnets. Applications and databases stay private, with outbound updates leaving through an outbound-only NAT gateway.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="ico">🔗</div><h3>Private AWS access</h3><p>VPC endpoints let workloads reach managed services such as S3 over the AWS network, never crossing the public internet.</p></div>
<div class="card"><div class="ico">📈</div><h3>Observability</h3><p>CloudWatch logs, metrics and alarms provide detection. Add CloudTrail for API auditing and GuardDuty for threat detection.</p></div>
</div>
<h4>Security groups vs network ACLs</h4>
<div class="tw"><table>
<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Security group</th><th>Network ACL</th></tr>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>Instance / network interface</td><td>Subnet</td></tr>
<tr><td>State</td><td>Stateful: return traffic is automatic</td><td>Stateless: allow both directions explicitly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rules</td><td>Allow rules only</td><td>Allow and deny, evaluated in order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Primary, fine-grained control</td><td>Coarse subnet guardrail or explicit block</td></tr>
</table></div>
<h4>Hardening checklist</h4>
<div class="card chk" id="chk">
<label><input type="checkbox"> MFA enabled for root and all privileged users</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> IAM roles with least-privilege policies, no long-lived keys</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> Databases and app servers in private subnets only</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> S3 Block Public Access on, access through a VPC endpoint</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> WAF in front of every public application</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> Encryption at rest and TLS in transit everywhere</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> CloudTrail, CloudWatch alarms and GuardDuty active</label>
<label><input type="checkbox"> Multi-AZ for data, with tested backups and recovery</label>
<div class="bar" aria-hidden="true"><i id="bar"></i></div><small id="pct" style="color:var(--mut)">Tick items to track your progress.</small>
</div>
<h4>Shared responsibility</h4>
<p class="lead" style="margin-top:0">AWS secures the cloud itself: hardware, facilities and the internals of managed services. You secure what you put in it: identities, configuration, network rules, data and applications.</p>
</section>

